{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>וַיִּקְרָא אֶל מֹשֶׁה וְגוֹ׳.</b> The alef of וַיִּקְרָא is small, as though it were written ״וַיִּקָּר,״ and the reason for this is to draw an equivalence between the prophecy of Moses and the prophecy of Balaam, concerning whom the expression ״וַיִּקָּר״ is used. And as the Sages said (Sifrei Berakha 39): ״וְלֹא קָם נָבִיא עוֹד בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל כְּמֹשֶׁה״ — 'there did not arise a prophet again in Israel like Moses,' but among the nations one did arise, and who was it? Balaam. And the meaning is not that Balaam was equal to Moses in prophecy, Heaven forbid; rather, the meaning is this: that Moses attained more than he was worthy of attaining based on his own predisposition, as it is written (Deuteronomy 34:10) ״אֲשֶׁר יְדָעוֹ ה׳״ — it does not say 'who knew HaShem,' but rather 'whom HaShem knew,' to tell you that from the side of his own predisposition he did not know HaShem in this manner; rather, HaShem knew him and granted him an added influx for Israel's sake — that is to say, in the merit of Israel. And all the other prophets of Israel, each one did not attain more than the measure of his predisposition; but among the nations of the world one arose for the honor of Israel who attained more than he was worthy of attaining based on his own predisposition. Now it is well known that whatever a prophet attains that is fitting for him according to his predisposition — that prophecy is bound to his very essence, and an acquisition bound to one's essence is never separated from him. But whatever he attains beyond the measure of his predisposition — that exists for him only as an occurrence and not in essence, and is solely a gift of Elohim. Therefore the expression ״וַיִּקָּר״ is used of both Moses and Balaam, to indicate that they were equal in this respect: that in both of them there was an attainment that occurred incidentally, beyond what was fitting for them from the side of their own essence." "And with this, what Rashi explained is settled — that ״וַיִּקָּר״ is an expression of coincidental occurrence and an expression of impurity. For one of those two alone would have sufficed to mark the distinction between ״וַיִּקָּר״ and ״וַיִּקְרָא,״ but since Rashi also holds that the prophecy of Moses was incidental and not essential from the side of his predisposition, the question arose for him: why was ״וַיִּקָּר״ not written entirely without an alef? Therefore he comes to resolve and say: because the expression ״וַיִּקָּר״ connotes coincidental occurrence — that is, the happening — and also connotes impurity, and this is not applicable at all to Moses, Heaven forbid. Therefore the alef was written in connection with Moses to remove him from the connotation of impurity associated with מִקְרֶה. Nevertheless, the alef was written small, to be read as ״וַיִּקָּר,״ to indicate that even the prophecy of Moses was coincidental and incidental from the side of its being beyond his predisposition, as stated above — but the reverse, to say it was associated with impurity, cannot be said at all. And it may be that this is the view of the Midrash, as concluded in the Yalkut (431) on this verse, and this is its language: 'One might think HaShem spoke with Moses for his own need — Scripture says, ״לֵאמֹר,״ for the need of Israel He spoke with him and not for his own need.' And this Midrash requires explanation, for how could it enter one's mind to say that the communication of all the commandments was for Moses' need alone — was he alone the one who heard ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״? Rather, it certainly speaks of the levels of prophecy — that Moses merited ״וַיִּקָּר,״ that is, an incidental prophecy that was added to him beyond the measure of his predisposition, such that HaShem spoke with him face to face in the merit of Israel and not on account of his own essence. And this is what it says: 'for the need of Israel He spoke with him' — for their need he merited this addition, and not for his own essence's need, as it is written ״וְלֹא קָם נָבִיא בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל,״ the bet serving as an expression of 'on account of.' Therefore, when Israel sinned, Moses was told ״לֶךְ רֵד״ — descend from your greatness, that is, from this added influx that was given him on account of Israel. And after the pardon, it was necessary to state that Moses returned to his former state, as it is written (Exodus 33:11) ״וְדִבֶּר ה׳ אֶל מֹשֶׁה פָּנִים אֶל פָּנִים,״ and this explanation is a precious thing." "And the Yalkut concludes why the young children begin their learning from the section of the sacrifices — just as the sacrifices are pure, so too the young children. And this is explained according to the approach concluded in the Yalkut on the portion of Pinchas (Numbers 28:3) ״כְּבָשִׂים בְּנֵי שָׁנָה״ — that they wash away a person's sins and render him like a one-year-old infant. On account of this, the small alef is a hint that the young ones should begin here, and this learning is at the beginning, as the alef is the head of the letters. Another matter: alef is an expression of learning, as in ״וַאֲאַלֶּפְךָ חָכְמָה״ (Job 33:33) — a hint that learning endures only in one who makes himself small. And likewise it hints that Moses merited this calling because he made himself small and fled from authority and said ״לֹא אִישׁ דְּבָרִים אָנֹכִי״ (Exodus 4:10), as concluded in the Yalkut (427)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:17.285892460Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}